\section{A good atlas near $D$}\label{sec:atlas}

\begin{lemma}[Good atlas]\label{lem:atlas}
There exist finitely many Darboux charts
$\{(U_\nu,\varphi_\nu)\}_{\nu=1}^{N}$ with coordinates
$(z_1^{(\nu)},\dots,z_n^{(\nu)})$, open $V_\nu\Subset U_\nu$, smooth
$\chi_\nu\colon M\to[0,1]$, and for every $\nu$ and every
$k\le r_\nu$ a real constant $\vartheta_{\nu,k}\in\R/2\pi\Z$ (already
absorbed in the coordinates), such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\normalfont(A1)] Each $U_\nu$ and each $V_\nu$ is connected;
$D\subset\bigcup_\nu V_\nu$.
\item[\normalfont(A2)] On $U_\nu$:
$D\cap U_\nu=\{z_1^{(\nu)}\cdots z_{r_\nu}^{(\nu)}=0\}$ and
$D_{\alpha_k}\cap U_\nu=\{z_k^{(\nu)}=0\}$ ($k\le r_\nu$); no other
$D_\alpha$ meets $U_\nu$.
\item[\normalfont(A3)] For every $\nu$ with
$D_\alpha\cap V_\nu\neq\emptyset$, $D_\alpha$ is a branch of chart
$\nu$ (equivalently: a component meets $V_\nu$ iff it is a branch of
chart $\nu$).
\item[\normalfont(A4)] $\supp\chi_\nu\subset V_\nu$, and
$\sum_\nu\chi_\nu=1$ on a neighbourhood of $D$.
\item[\normalfont(A5)] Whenever $D_\gamma$ is a branch of both charts
$\nu,\mu$ (i.e.\ $D_\gamma\cap V_\nu\neq\emptyset$,
$D_\gamma\cap V_\mu\neq\emptyset$), with branch indices
$k=k(\gamma,\nu)$, $l=k(\gamma,\mu)$: on the overlap
$U_\nu\cap U_\mu$ one has
$z_k^{(\nu)}=h_{\nu\mu}\,z_l^{(\mu)}$ with $h_{\nu\mu}$ smooth
nonvanishing, and
\[
h_{\nu\mu}(q)\ \in\ \R_{>0}
\qquad\text{for every } q\in
V_\nu\cap V_\mu\cap D_\gamma .
\]
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(A1)--(A2): By the SNC definition each point has a Darboux chart with
$D\cap U=\{x_1\cdots x_r=0\}$ and the components through the centre
equal to coordinate hyperplanes; shrink the chart to avoid the
finitely many other components (each $D_\alpha$ is closed) and to be
a connected Darboux ball. By compactness of $D$, finitely many such
charts $(U_\nu,\varphi_\nu)$ with precompact shrinkings
$V_\nu\Subset U_\nu$ cover $D$; take $V_\nu$ connected (e.g.\
slightly smaller Darboux balls).

(A3): If $D_\alpha\cap V_\nu\neq\emptyset$, then
$D_\alpha\cap U_\nu\neq\emptyset$, so $D_\alpha$ is one of the
branches. The converse is automatic from the chart construction: each
branch hyperplane of chart $\nu$ meets every open set with compact
closure in $U_\nu$... not automatic: arrange it by shrinking each
$V_\nu$ slightly toward the centre along the divisor: the branch
$\{z_k^{(\nu)}=0\}$ passes through the centre
$p_\nu=\varphi_\nu^{-1}(0)\in V_\nu$ (charts centred on $D$: we may
choose each chart centre on $D$... if the centre is not on the
branch $k$, the branch hyperplane still meets any neighbourhood of
the centre? NO: a hyperplane not through the centre misses a small
ball. But the hyperplane meets $V_\nu$ or not; if not, drop it from
the branch list of the chart (define the effective
$r_\nu$ accordingly; note the corresponding component then does not
meet $V_\nu$ at all, consistent); after this relabelling, every
branch of chart $\nu$ meets $V_\nu$, giving (A3): in fact every
coordinate hyperplane $\{z_k^{(\nu)}=0\}$
meets $V_\nu$ since it contains the centre $p_\nu\in V_\nu$ if the
centre is on $D$; we choose all chart centres on $D$ from the start,
so (A3) holds with the same $r_\nu$.

\noindent
Also note for later: with centres at $p_\nu\in D$, in the coordinates
of chart $\nu$ one has $z_k^{(\nu)}(p_\nu)=0$ for all
